{"name":"Ether","hex":"#98b2b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ether","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The god-personification of the bright, glowing upper air of heaven. He is the Roman counterpart of Aether."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A unit of the Ethereum digital currency, ETH."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ether"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ether","blocks":[{"text":"In organic chemistry, ethers are a class of compounds that contain an ether group, a single oxygen atom bonded to two separate carbon atoms, each part of an organyl group. They have the general formula R−O−R′, where R and R′ represent the organyl groups. Ethers can again be classified into two varieties: if the organyl groups are the same on both sides of the oxygen atom, then it is a simple or symmetrical ether, whereas if they are different, the ethers are called mixed or unsymmetrical ethers. A typical example of the first group is the solvent and anaesthetic diethyl ether, commonly referred to simply as \"ether\". Ethers are common in organic chemistry and even more prevalent in biochemistry, as they are common linkages in carbohydrates and lignin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Ether-%28general%29.svg/330px-Ether-%28general%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ether","wikidata":"Q103230"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/CSD_CIF_CANZOG10.png/500px-CSD_CIF_CANZOG10.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/CSD_CIF_CANZOG10.png/500px-CSD_CIF_CANZOG10.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of VCl3(thf)3.[8].mw-parser-output .legend{page-break-inside:avoid;break-inside:avoid-column}.mw-parser-output .legend-color{display